the virology section allows you to take  the data that was produced by the  pipeline and visualize it  using this interface  this interface shows the reference  genome of the virus the rna virus and  then it shows all the passages here this  right here is  coverage and the coverage is represented  by a line  according to every passage  so you can add and remove passages to  display only the ones that you would be  interested in seeing  you can also go in and check more  specifically  the coverage by number  and it is important because our  calculation of mutation rate is based on  the coverage you can also change the bin  size and see  a higher resolution  by bin size the bin size is by number of  nucleotides  all of the information about every  aspect of this visualization  can be seen by hovering over the eye  button  now if you want to look deeper  for example  this area or this area  they display that there is a pretty high  rate of mutation  and the high rate of mutation  is also adjustable by this slider  so you can make it more sensitive for an  area of mutation that you want to see  of course  more mutations are going to be revealed  by lowering  the  red area  now if we want to check this area we are  opening now a view that shows the  nucleotide  level of resolution  and  you can also move  by bins  using these arrows  now once we've seen  this area of interest we can hover over  it and see  which mutations occur  which nucleotide it is which position  the sequence variability coverage and  like i said which  letter  the mutation occurs  so  now we can close this and also check the  different types of mutations  first of all we are displaying  synonymous and non-synonymous at this  point so we can turn one or the other  off  to check um which remain  and now these are the types of mutations  that our system  calculates  based on adaptation to simplify  beneficial  are the areas that  are beneficial for viral adaptation to  the host  neutral  means that they don't  they occur randomly  detrimental  displays the mutations that  are  not beneficial  to the viral adaptation and lethal means  those mutations actually  change the amino acid to a stop codon  so  those are special types of mutations  now if we only look for example at the  beneficial ones  and we can see how these mutations  are mapped on the 3d structure of the  protein in this protein right here and  we can  make  this image bigger  we can take a look at how these  mutations are mapped on the 3d structure  and  we can also play with the different  views  over here so  this right here shows me  the amino acids colored  in the same way that i see them right  here  and i can also hover over them and see  them displayed  on the 3d structure  as you saw i can also color by ratio by  mutation rate  now i can also render  in spheres if i want to look at that  view  so for example i'm interested in this  area i can see  right here and you also see the area  that is covered in this view  and then  displaying different uh types of  mutations um is done  over here  uh so for example i see this cluster  right here that could be of interest to  me  and that is a way to  um  to see the data in a more visual format  now you can also read a little bit about  this protein and by clicking on this you  actually see the  copy and paste version of the amino  acids if you want to take it further  and  research  so  this  shows  different proteins  the different proteins  could be again viewed you could see  subproteins  and  we can also  see this help  that explains a little bit more about  this visualization tool
 

